1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,530
Jeanne: Hi Thomas, how are you enjoying the course so far?

2
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:06,190
Thomas: Yeah, I think it's good.

3
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:09,437
Jeanne: Remind me - why did you decide to study sports science?

4
00:00:09,437 --> 00:00:13,570
Didn't you want to be a professional athlete when you were at school?

5
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:17,860
Thomas: Yeah - that was my goal, and all my classmates assumed I would achieve it;

6
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:19,290
they thought I was brilliant.

7
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,360
Jeanne: That must have been a nice feeling.

8
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,360
Thomas: Mm, I thought I could win anything

9
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:26,810
There was no one who could run faster than me.

10
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:30,840
Jeanne: Exactly - so what happened? Did your mum

11
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:32,970
and dad want you to be more 'academic'?

12
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:37,160
Thomas: Not at all. Perhaps they should have pushed me harder, though.

13
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,290
Jeanne: What do you mean?

14
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:40,670
Thomas: I think I should have practised more.

15
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:42,150
Jeanne: What makes you say that?

16
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:45,850
Thomas: Well, I went out to Kenya for a couple of weeks to train...

17
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:48,470
Jeanne: Really! I didn't know that.

18
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:52,634
Thomas: I was chosen to go there out of loads of kids

19
00:00:52,634 --> 00:00:56,798
and run with some of the top teenage athletes in the world.

20
00:00:56,798 --> 00:00:59,227
And... I was so calm about it.

21
00:00:59,227 --> 00:01:02,004
I just kept thinking how fortunate I was.

22
00:01:02,004 --> 00:01:04,086
What a great chance this was!

23
00:01:04,086 --> 00:01:06,862
Everyone back home was so proud of me.

24
00:01:06,862 --> 00:01:11,720
But once we started competing, I very quickly realised I wasn't good enough.

25
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,400
Jeanne: That must have been a huge shock.

26
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:18,620
Thomas: I thought 'this can't be happening'! I was used to winning.

27
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,330
Jeanne: I'm sorry to hear that.

28
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:22,600
Thomas: It's OK. I'm over it now

29
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,516
and I think it's much better to do a university course

30
00:01:26,516 --> 00:01:31,230
and this one has such a variety of sports-related areas. It's going to be good.

31
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:37,230
Jeanne: Oh, I agree - I chose it because of that.

32
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:43,450
Thomas: So Jeanne - have you thought of any ideas for the discussion session next week on technology and sport?

33
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,660
Jeanne: We have to cover more than one sport, don't we?

34
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:47,350
Thomas: Yeah.

35
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:51,655
Jeanne: You know - we always think technology is about the future,

36
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:55,960
but we could gather some ideas about past developments in sport.

37
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:59,243
Thomas: Look at early types of equipment perhaps?

38
00:01:59,243 --> 00:02:03,347
Uh, I remember reading something about table tennis bats once -

39
00:02:03,347 --> 00:02:07,040
how they ended up being covered with pimpled rubber.

40
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:10,060
Jeanne: Cos they were just wooden at first, I'd imagine.

41
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:12,337
Thomas: Yeah. In about the 1920s,

42
00:02:12,337 --> 00:02:18,030
a factory was making rolls of the rubber in bulk for something like horse harnesses.

43
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:19,250
Jeanne: Really!

44
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:24,230
Thomas: Yeah - and someone realised that it'd make a perfect covering for the wooden bats.

45
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:28,860
Jeanne: So what about cricket - that's had a few innovative changes

46
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:32,580
Maybe the pads they wear on their legs?

47
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:35,935
Thomas: I don't think they've changed much but,

48
00:02:35,935 --> 00:02:42,645
I'm just looking on the internet... and it says that when the first cricket helmet came in, in 1978,

49
00:02:42,645 --> 00:02:46,020
the Australian batsman who first wore it was booed

50
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:49,020
and jeered by people watching because it was so ugly!

51
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:53,760
Jeanne: Wow, players have to protect themselves from getting hurt

52
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:56,280
I mean everyone wears one now.

53
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,190
Thomas: Mm, unlike the cycle helmet.

54
00:02:59,190 --> 00:03:02,940
Jeanne: Well, unless you're a professional, but you're right

55
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,120
many ordinary bikers don't wear a helmet.

56
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,900
Thomas: Hey, look at these pictures of original helmet designs

57
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,320
This one looks like an upside-down bowl!

58
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:18,580
Jeanne: Yet, the woman's laughing - she's so proud to be wearing it!

59
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:24,030
Thomas: It says serious cyclists ended up with wet hair from all the hard exercise.

60
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:27,680
Jeanne: I guess that's why they have large air vents in them now

61
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,240
so that the skin can breathe more easily.

62
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,700
Thomas: OK, so we've done helmets

63
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:39,620
What about golf balls or better still golf clubs - they've changed a lot.

64
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:43,580
Jeanne: Yeah - I remember my great grandfather telling me that

65
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,061
because a club was made entirely of wood,

66
00:03:46,061 --> 00:03:49,640
it would easily break and players had to get another.

67
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,110
Thomas: There's no wood at all in them now, is there?

68
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:54,530
Jeanne: No - they're much more powerful.

69
00:03:54,530 --> 00:03:56,950
Thomas: The same must be true of hockey sticks.

70
00:03:56,950 --> 00:04:02,434
Jeanne: I don't think so because players still use wooden sticks today.

71
00:04:02,434 --> 00:04:06,100
What it does say here, though

72
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:10,450
is that when the game started you had to produce a stick yourself.

73
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:12,940
Thomas: I guess they just weren't being manufactured

74
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:17,930
So, one more perhaps. What about football?

75
00:04:17,930 --> 00:04:22,400
Jeanne: Well, I know the first balls were made of animal skin.

76
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:26,991
Thomas: Yeah, they covered them with pieces of leather that were stitched together,

77
00:04:26,991 --> 00:04:30,170
but... the balls let in water when it rained.

78
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:32,860
Jeanne: Oh, that would have made them much heavier.

79
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:36,660
Thomas: That's right. You can imagine the damage to players

80
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,100
necks when the ball was headed.

81
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:40,230
Jeanne: How painful that must have been!

82
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:46,000
Thomas: Yeah, well, I think we can put together some useful ideas...
